# Plan Writing Rules

Apply these whenever you create or edit a cleanup, fix or refactor plan for code that already exists, including in Plan mode.

A specification for a NEW feature is not a plan. It goes to `docs/requirements/{N}_{name}.md` and follows `docs/requirements/0_TEMPLATE.md`, not the rules below.

## 1. Where plans live

- Every plan is ONE Markdown file in `docs/plans/` (relative to repo root).
- Never put a plan in a system or internal directory (for example `.cursor/`, temp folders, or OS paths).
- Use flat files. Do not build nested folder trees for a plan.

## 2. One plan, one file

- Create exactly one plan file. That file is the only source of truth.
- Do NOT also create a second or "internal" plan and keep two copies in sync.
- If Plan mode keeps its own plan, the `docs/plans/` file is the real one. Do not copy its content into another doc.

## 3. Write in simple English

- Use short, everyday words and short sentences.
- If a simple word works, use it instead of a fancy one.
- English only, in every file in this repo.

## 4. Structure (in this order)

1. **TL;DR** — max 3 bullets.
2. **Decisions** — a table: Decision | Choice | Why (<=10 words). "Why" is the reason the choice is good on its own, not why it beats an older idea.
3. **Changes** — a checklist, one line per file. No paragraphs.
4. **Details** — any long reasoning goes here, inside a collapsible `<details>` block.

Rules: use bullet fragments, not paragraphs, above "Details". Max 8 bullets per section.

## 5. The plan shows only how things are NOW

- The plan is standalone. The reader never saw the old version.
- Write only the current decision. Never mention past versions of the plan.
- Never write "changed from X", "was X", "X not Y", "instead of X", "previously", or "updated to".
- If asked to change 1 to 2, the doc just says 2 — as if it always said 2.

## 6. Revising a plan

- Edit the file in place. Never reprint the full plan in chat.
- In chat, reply only with: (1) a short changelog (section + one-line reason), and (2) open questions.
- Make the smallest edit that does the job. Do not reword, reorder, or reformat unchanged content. Keep existing wording exactly, except the lines the change needs.
- Say which sections you did not touch.

## 7. Only change what is needed

- Change only what the request asks for.
- Do not edit a section just because you saw it. No far-fetched or "just in case" changes.
- Never edit something only to look productive or to make the response bigger.
- If nothing needs to change, change nothing.

## 8. Claims must be checked, not guessed

- Mark a symptom "reproduced" only after you actually ran the app and saw it. Otherwise say it is read from the code.
- Cite the file and line you read: `Sources/EasyWrite/Replacer.swift:31`.
- This app cannot be exercised on CI or on a non-Apple-Silicon machine, so "reproduced" means a manual run on a supported Mac.

## 9. Before writing a new plan

- If anything is unclear, ask up to 5 multiple-choice questions first, then write the plan.
